===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# [[good|Good]]; in acceptable, if not excellent condition.
#: {{ux|en|The car is '''all right'''. It gets me there, anyway.}}
# In good [[health]], [[unharmed]].
#: {{ux|en|I had a headache earlier, but now I'm '''all right'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1903|author=William Brodie; Carl Jungk; Francis Edward Stewart|title=The Therapeutic Gazette|page=536
|passage=The dread of most etherizers is a quiet breather who may stop breathing without his notice, and requires the almost constant presence of his ear at the patient's mouth to be sure he or she is '''all right'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=20|passage=&lsquo;No. I only opened the door a foot and put my head in. The street lamps shine into that room. I could see him. He was '''all right'''. Sleeping like a great grampus. Poor, poor chap.’}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=1971
|author=w:Lyndon Johnson
|chapter=The Beginning
|title=w:The Vantage Point
|url=https://archive.org/details/vantagepointpers00john/
|publisher=w:Holt, Reinhart & Winston
|ISBN=0-03-084492-4
|LCCN=74-102146
|OCLC=1067880747
|page=16
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/vantagepointpers00john/page/16/
|text=Next I called my dear friend {{w|Nellie Connally}} in Dallas. We had been close to Nellie and John for many years, sharing moments of sorrow and happiness alike. She reported that the surgeon had just finished operating and that John was going to be '''all right'''.}}
#* {{see more citations|en}}

====Usage notes====
The comparative form "more all right" is used, but rarely. An example would be where another speaker had used the phrase "all right" and repeating it and extending it enhanced the continuity of the conversation. ''I didn't feel '''all right''' earlier today, so I took a power-nap. Now I feel even '''more all right''' than I normally do.''

